    "blocks_jRKrzC": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_0029bb1_FQrqMG": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_0029bb1",
          "name": "Heading and text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How is a diamond cut?",
            "text": "",
            "heading_size_desktop": 32,
            "text_size_desktop": 16,
            "padding_left_desktop": 0,
            "padding_right_desktop": 0,
            "spacing_desktop": 20,
            "heading_size_mobile": 24,
            "text_size_mobile": 14,
            "padding_left_mobile": 0,
            "padding_right_mobile": 0,
            "spacing_mobile": 15,
            "text_alignment": "center",
            "heading_color": "#000000",
            "text_color": "#000000"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
        "ai_gen_block_ae447c2_XqY3jt": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_ae447c2",
          "settings": {
            "padding_top_desktop": 0,
            "padding_bottom_desktop": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 0,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
        "ai_gen_block_0029bb1_aWRfVN": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_0029bb1",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "",
            "text": "<p>Before a diamond becomes the finished stone seen in a piece of jewellery, it passes through a highly considered cutting process. Every decision, from the first assessment of the rough diamond to the final polish, affects how the stone will handle light, reveal its shape and express its character.</p><p><strong>Planning:</strong> The rough diamond is studied to understand its natural structure, proportions and possibilities. This stage determines the most suitable cut and how best to preserve beauty, brilliance and weight.</p><p><strong>Marking: </strong>Once the cutting plan is decided, guide lines are marked onto the stone. These markings act as a roadmap for the cutter.</p><p><strong>Cleaving: </strong>The diamond is carefully divided using specialist equipment, often with a laser or diamond-edged saw. This separates the rough stone into workable sections.</p><p><strong>Shaping:</strong> The diamond is then formed into its early outline. This stage establishes the basic silhouette of the stone, whether round, oval, pear, emerald, cushion or another shape.</p><p><strong>Faceting: </strong>The facets are cut into the diamond with precision. These polished planes are what allow light to enter, reflect and return through the stone, creating brilliance, fire and sparkle.</p><p><strong>Polishing: </strong>Each facet is refined until its surface, angle and symmetry are clean and exact. Even small variations can affect the way a diamond performs.</p><p><strong>Cleaning:</strong> The stone is cleaned to remove any residue from the cutting and polishing process.</p><p><strong>Final Assessment:</strong> The completed diamond is inspected and graded according to the 4Cs: cut, colour, clarity and carat weight.</p><p>Understanding how a diamond is cut helps explain why two stones of the same shape can look and perform very differently. Cut quality has a major influence on the life, brightness and overall beauty of a diamond.</p>",
            "heading_size_desktop": 24,
            "text_size_desktop": 18,
            "padding_left_desktop": 380,
            "padding_right_desktop": 380,
            "spacing_desktop": 0,
            "heading_size_mobile": 18,
            "text_size_mobile": 14,
            "padding_left_mobile": 40,
            "padding_right_mobile": 40,
            "spacing_mobile": 0,
            "text_alignment": "center",
            "heading_color": "#000000",
            "text_color": "#000000"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
